# Target C — diameter-vertex-cover repacking criterion for an inactive chamber

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only structural reduction; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Base:** `231fcc455f19c54974a3ffd1aaa27f00ca436a86`.  
**Dependencies:** C65 full actual diameter visibility; canonical Woodall diameter-topology classification; canonical convex inactive-chamber geometry; the strict interior diameter lemma from the audited inactive-exchange module.

The current inactive-chamber exchange theorem deletes one loaded vertex and asks for a
local `k -> k+1` repacking. There is a stronger global mechanism that does not require
the replacement to remain triangle-free and does not need a contact-count comparison.

Delete a **vertex cover of the full actual diameter graph** and replace those deleted
loaded vertices, together with all isolates assigned to one protected inactive chamber,
by the same number of unit-separated points deep inside that chamber. Every old
diameter pair then disappears. Every new point lies in the interior of the old source
hull and therefore participates in no diameter pair. The resulting source has strict
diameter `<D`, contradicting global minimality directly.

Woodall's exact diameter-graph classification makes the required deletion set only
linear:

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)\le\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil
\le {\pi D\over2}+1,
}
\tag{1}
\]

where `tau(H)` is the minimum vertex-cover number of the actual diameter graph and
`q=|V(H)|` is the loaded-terminal count.

Thus an internal inactive chamber closes C-II as soon as its protected unit-packing
capacity exceeds its present isolate population by `tau(H)` slots. This is a different
closure target from the current one-extra-point exchange: it trades a linear chamber
repacking for deletion of **all** old diameter pairs, after which triangle-freeness of
the competitor is irrelevant.

---

## 1. Setup and protected packing capacity

Let

\[
A\subset\mathbb R^2,
\qquad |A|=n,
\qquad \min_{x\ne y}|x-y|\ge1,
\qquad \operatorname{diam}A=D=D(n)
\]

be the lexicographically normalized hypothetical triangle-free global minimizer.
C65 identifies the complete actual diameter graph

\[
H=(B,E_H),
\qquad B=V(H),
\qquad q=|B|,
\]

and every source point outside the nonisolated positive-contact core has all pairwise
distances strictly between `1` and `D`.

Let `C` be a convex inactive chamber with assigned contact-isolated source set

\[
Z_C\subset A\cap\operatorname{int}C,
\qquad z_C:=|Z_C|,
\]

such that every source point not in `Z_C` lies outside `int C`. This is the protected
chamber situation used by the canonical/audited inactive-chamber modules.

Put

\[
C_{-1}:=\{x\in C:\operatorname{dist}(x,\partial C)>1\}.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Define its protected unit-packing capacity

\[
M_1(C):=\sup\Bigl\{|Y|:
Y\subset C_{-1},
\ |y-y'|\ge1\ (y\ne y')\Bigr\}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

The supremum is a finite integer in the only sense needed below: if
`M_1(C)>=N`, there exists a unit-separated `N`-point subset of `C_{-1}`.

For every `y in C_{-1}` and every source point `a notin int C`, convexity gives

\[
|y-a|>1.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Indeed the segment from `y` to `a` meets `partial C`, so its length is at least
`dist(y,partial C)>1`.

---

## 2. A diameter vertex cover is enough to force strict global descent

Let `U subseteq B` be any vertex cover of `H`; that is, every actual diameter edge has
at least one endpoint in `U`. Suppose there exists

\[
Y\subset C_{-1}
\]

with

\[
|Y|=z_C+|U|,
\tag{2.1}
\]

and `Y` unit-separated.

Define

\[
A':=(A\setminus(Z_C\cup U))\cup Y.
\tag{2.2}
\]

### Theorem 2.1 — protected cover-repacking contradiction

Under the preceding hypotheses,

\[
\boxed{
|A'|=|A|,
\qquad
\min_{x\ne y\in A'}|x-y|\ge1,
\qquad
\operatorname{diam}A'<D.
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

Hence such a set `Y` cannot exist in a global diameter minimizer.

### Proof

Cardinality is immediate from (2.1).

For unit separation, retained old source points remain mutually unit-separated. New
points are mutually unit-separated by hypothesis. Every retained old source point lies
outside `int C`, because all original source points in `int C` assigned to this inactive
chamber are in `Z_C`; equation (1.3) therefore gives strict distance greater than one
between `Y` and every retained old point.

It remains to prove strict diameter decrease.

Because `U` is a vertex cover of the **complete actual** diameter graph, no retained
old-old pair has distance `D`. Thus every pair in

\[
A\setminus(Z_C\cup U)
\]

has distance strictly less than `D`.

Every point of `Y` lies in `C_{-1} subset int C`. The chamber lies in the force hull,
which lies in the full source hull

\[
K:=conv(A).
\]

Hence `Y subset int K`. The standard strict interior lemma says

\[
|x-y|<D
\qquad(x\in K,\ y\in int K).
\tag{2.4}
\]

For completeness: `K subseteq closed B(x,D)` for each `x in K` because
`diam K=diam A=D`; an interior point of `K` cannot lie on the boundary circle of that
disk, since every neighborhood of such a circle point contains points outside the
disk. Thus (2.4) holds.

Equation (2.4) makes every new-old distance and every new-new distance strictly below
`D`. All retained old-old distances are also strictly below `D`. Since `A'` is finite,
its maximum pair distance is strictly below `D`. This proves (2.3), contradicting the
definition of `D(n)`. `square`

Notice what is **not** required: `A'` need not be triangle-free, and no contact count
is compared. The contradiction is direct global diameter descent.

---

## 3. Woodall gives a half-terminal vertex cover

Canonical C-WOODALL says the actual diameter graph has exactly one of two forms.

### 3.1 Caterpillar-forest case

If `H` is a forest, it is bipartite. Either color class is a vertex cover, so choosing
the smaller class gives

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)\le\left\lfloor{q\over2}\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{3.1}
\]

No caterpillar-specific refinement is needed for this bound.

### 3.2 Connected odd-unicyclic case

Suppose the unique cycle has length

\[
r=2k+1,
\]

and every remaining loaded vertex is a pendant leaf attached directly to one cycle
vertex. Let `ell_i>=0` be the number of leaves attached to cycle vertex `i`, and put

\[
L:=\sum_{i=1}^r\ell_i,
\qquad q=r+L.
\]

There are `r` maximum independent sets of size `k` in the odd cycle, obtained by
cyclically rotating one alternating pattern. By symmetry each cycle vertex belongs to
exactly `k` of those `r` sets. Therefore the average leaf weight

\[
w(I):=\sum_{i\in I}\ell_i
\]

over these maximum cycle-independent sets is

\[
{1\over r}\sum_I w(I)
={k\over r}L
\le {L\over2},
\tag{3.2}
\]

with strict inequality when `L>0`.

If `L=0`, the graph is the bare odd cycle and

\[
\tau(H)=\tau(C_{2k+1})=k+1
 =\left\lceil{2k+1\over2}\right\rceil.
\tag{3.2a}
\]

If `L>0`, choose one such `I` with

\[
w(I)<L/2.
\]

Take as a vertex cover

- all cycle vertices outside `I`; and
- every pendant leaf attached to a vertex of `I`.

The cycle edges are covered because `I` is independent. Every pendant edge is covered
because either its cycle endpoint is in the cover or its leaf is. The cover size is

\[
(k+1)+w(I).
\]

Since `w(I)` is an integer strictly below `L/2`, this is at most

\[
\left\lceil{2k+1+L\over2}\right\rceil
=\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Thus in both Woodall cases

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)\le\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

Finally the loaded vertices occur in convex position and are mutually unit-separated
around the force hull, so

\[
q\le\operatorname{per}(F)\le\pi D.
\tag{3.5}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)
\le\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil
\le {\pi D\over2}+1.
}
\tag{3.6}
\]

(The harmless final `+1` avoids parity notation.)

---

## 4. Exact inactive-branch capacity obstruction

Take `U` to be a minimum vertex cover. Theorem 2.1 gives the survivor condition

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C)
\le z_C+\tau(H)-1.
}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Equivalently, define the protected spare capacity

\[
\operatorname{spare}(C):=M_1(C)-z_C.
\]

Every triangle-free global minimizer with such an inactive chamber must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{spare}(C)<\tau(H)
\le\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

Thus any theorem proving

\[
M_1(C)\ge z_C+\tau(H)
\tag{4.3}
\]

for the canonical macroscopic inactive chamber closes that branch immediately by a
same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor.

A topology-free sufficient condition is

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C)
\ge z_C+\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{4.4}
\]

Because `q=O(D)`, the remaining chamber problem is now explicitly a **linear spare
packing-capacity theorem**, not a one-extra-point theorem and not a force-transfer
theorem.

---

## 5. A lattice lower bound records the exact residual packing gap

Let `Lambda` be the unit triangular lattice of covolume

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

Average the count

\[
|(t+\Lambda)\cap C_{-1}|
\]

over one translation fundamental domain. Its mean is

\[
{\operatorname{area}(C_{-1})\over a_0}.
\]

Therefore some translate gives

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C)
\ge
\left\lceil{\operatorname{area}(C_{-1})\over a_0}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{5.1}
\]

Combining with (4.1) gives a necessary survivor inequality

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{area}(C_{-1})\over a_0}
< z_C+\tau(H).
}
\tag{5.2}
\]

This does not yet contradict the canonical chamber Oler estimate. It identifies the
remaining quantitative obstruction correctly: a near-capacity chamber may store a
linear boundary surplus above the bulk triangular-lattice count.

For a convex chamber with inradius greater than one, inner-parallel coarea gives

\[
\operatorname{area}(C)-\operatorname{area}(C_{-1})
\le\operatorname{per}(C).
\tag{5.3}
\]

If

\[
\sigma_C^{\rm Oler}
:={\operatorname{area}(C)\over a_0}
+{\operatorname{per}(C)\over2}+1-z_C,
\tag{5.4}
\]

then (5.2)--(5.3) imply the explicit necessary upper allowance

\[
\sigma_C^{\rm Oler}
<
\left({1\over a_0}+{1\over2}\right)
\operatorname{per}(C)
+\tau(H)+1.
\tag{5.5}
\]

This is consistent with the canonical `O(D)` chamber slack and therefore is a handoff,
not a closure. Any improvement of the protected lattice/container lower bound by a
linear boundary term can now be compared directly with `tau(H)`.

---

## 6. Scope and hostile audit

- **Full cardinality:** exactly `z_C+|U|` points are deleted and exactly that many are
  inserted.
- **All source points:** no isolate is silently discarded; every deleted isolate is
  replaced one-for-one before the extra `|U|` slots corresponding to loaded vertices.
- **Complete diameter support:** C65 is essential. Covering only a selected positive
  diameter subgraph would not remove all old distance-`D` pairs.
- **New diameter pairs:** impossible because every replacement point is in `int K`.
- **Retained diameter pairs:** impossible because `U` covers the complete actual
  diameter graph.
- **Cross-boundary unit separation:** depth `>1` in `C_{-1}` is essential.
- **Triangle-freeness:** intentionally not required for the competitor, because the
  contradiction is `diam A'<D(n)`, not lexicographic contact minimization.
- **Contact-isolated affine kernel:** unused. Isolate coordinates never enter the
  Section-57 core rigidity theorem.
- **Exterior-cap branch:** not claimed here unless the same protected-depth hypothesis
  relative to all retained source points is separately established. The theorem is
  aimed at the canonical internal macroscopic inactive chamber.
- **Theorem authority:** unchanged. This is proof-only.

**Disposition:** exact inactive-branch reduction. C-II remains open. To eliminate the
internal macroscopic chamber it now suffices to prove the lower bound
`spare(C) >= tau(H)`; the topology-free sufficient target is
`spare(C) >= ceil(q/2)`, and the number of slots required is at most
`pi D/2+O(1)`.
